# 1. Your application on EC2 experiences traffic spikes only during business hours. How would you optimize cost and performance?

Since the traffic increases only during business hours, I would not keep the same number of EC2 instances running all day because it would increase costs.

I would use Auto Scaling Group (ASG) with a Load Balancer.

During business hours, Auto Scaling automatically launches more EC2 instances when CPU utilization or request count increases.

After business hours, it terminates the extra instances, keeping only the minimum required instances running.

This improves application performance during high traffic and reduces AWS costs during low traffic.

> You can answer:
EC2 – To host the application.
Auto Scaling Group (ASG) – To automatically increase or decrease the number of instances.
Application Load Balancer (ALB) – To distribute incoming traffic across multiple EC2 instances.
CloudWatch – To monitor CPU, memory (if configured), or request metrics and trigger scaling policies.
IAM – To securely manage permissions for AWS resources.

> Real Example
Suppose your company is an online banking application.
9 AM – 6 PM: Many users log in.
Auto Scaling increases instances from 2 → 8.
After 6 PM: Traffic decreases.
Auto Scaling reduces instances from 8 → 2.

Q1. Why not keep 10 EC2 instances running all the time?
Answer: Because traffic is low after business hours, keeping all instances running would increase AWS costs unnecessarily.

Q2. What metric can Auto Scaling use?
Answer: Common metrics include:
CPU Utilization
Request Count per Target (with ALB)
Network In/Out
Custom CloudWatch Metrics

Q3. Which service distributes traffic among EC2 instances?
Answer: Application Load Balancer (ALB).



# Q2. Your EC2 instance crashed unexpectedly. What steps would you take to identify the issue and restore service quickly?
First, I would check the EC2 instance status in the AWS Console to see if there are any status check failures.

Then, I would check the CloudWatch metrics like CPU utilization, memory (if configured), and disk usage to understand what happened.

Next, I would look at the system logs and application logs to identify the root cause.

If the instance is not recoverable, I would launch a new EC2 instance from an AMI or use an Auto Scaling Group to automatically replace the failed instance.

Finally, I would verify that the application is running correctly and monitor it to make sure the issue does not happen again.
> Real Example

Imagine an e-commerce website.
CPU suddenly reaches 100%
Website becomes unavailable

You would:
Check EC2 Status Checks
Check CloudWatch metrics
SSH into the instance and inspect logs
Restart the application or web server if needed

Q1. Which AWS service helps monitor EC2?
Answer: Amazon CloudWatch.

Q2. Which service can automatically replace a failed EC2 instance?
Answer: Auto Scaling Group (ASG).

Q3. Where do you check Linux system logs?
Answer:
Amazon Linux/RHEL: /var/log/messages or journalctl
Ubuntu: /var/log/syslog


# You have deployed your application on an EC2 instance. The application has to fetch some images from the internet, but it is not able to fetch them. What might be the possible issues?

If my application running on an EC2 instance cannot fetch images from the internet, I would first check whether the instance has internet connectivity.

Then I would verify the Security Group and Network ACL rules to ensure outbound traffic is allowed.

I would also check whether the EC2 instance is in a public subnet with an Internet Gateway, or if it is in a private subnet, whether it has access through a NAT Gateway.

Finally, I would verify DNS resolution, application configuration, and firewall settings to identify the root cause.

Q1. Why does a private EC2 instance need a NAT Gateway?
Answer:
A private EC2 instance has no direct internet access. A NAT Gateway allows it to make outbound internet requests (such as downloading updates or fetching images) while still preventing inbound internet connections.

Q2. Which AWS component provides internet access to a public subnet?
Answer:
Internet Gateway (IGW).

Q3. If Security Group outbound traffic is blocked, what happens?
Answer:
The EC2 instance cannot send requests to the internet, so the application cannot fetch images or call external APIs.

Q4. Can Security Groups block outbound traffic?
Answer:
Yes. Although the default outbound rule allows all traffic, if it has been modified or restricted, outbound requests can fail.

# 4. You accidentally deleted data from an S3 bucket. How do you recover it?

The recovery method depends on whether S3 Versioning or Backup was enabled.

If S3 Versioning is enabled, I can restore the previous version of the deleted object.

If AWS Backup or Cross-Region Replication is configured, I can recover the data from the backup or replica.

If none of these are enabled, the deleted object cannot be recovered. To avoid this in the future, I would enable Versioning, Backup, and MFA Delete for important buckets.

> Real Example
Suppose your company stores:
employee.csv

A developer accidentally deletes it.
Since Versioning is enabled:

employee.csv
Version 1
Version 2
Delete Marker

You remove the Delete Marker or restore the previous version.
The file is available again without data loss.

Q1. What is S3 Versioning?

Answer:
S3 Versioning keeps multiple versions of the same object. If an object is deleted or overwritten, earlier versions can be restored.

Q2. What is a Delete Marker?

Answer:
When Versioning is enabled, deleting an object creates a Delete Marker instead of permanently removing all versions. The older versions remain available and can be restored.

Q3. Can you recover an S3 object if Versioning is disabled?

Answer:
Only if you have another backup, such as AWS Backup or a replicated copy. Otherwise, the deleted object cannot be recovered.

Q4. How can you prevent accidental deletion of S3 data?
Enable S3 Versioning
Use AWS Backup
Enable MFA Delete (for critical data)
Use least-privilege IAM policies
Configure Cross-Region Replication if business continuity requires it


# 5. How do you connect an on-premises data center to AWS?

We can connect an on-premises data center to AWS using AWS Site-to-Site VPN or AWS Direct Connect.

Site-to-Site VPN is used over the public internet. It is secure, easy to set up, and cost-effective.
AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection between the data center and AWS. It offers higher bandwidth, lower latency, and more consistent performance.

> Real Example
Suppose a bank has:
Data center in Mumbai
Application running in AWS

The bank wants both environments to communicate securely.

Option 1: Use Site-to-Site VPN for secure encrypted communication over the internet.
Option 2: If the bank needs high-speed, low-latency, and stable connectivity, use AWS Direct Connect.

